# EDGE-LOG cloud logrotate template (item C, 2026-09-25) -- installed by install.sh to
# /etc/logrotate.d/edgelog with the same __EDGELOG_*__ placeholder substitution the
# systemd unit templates in this directory use (see install.sh's `sed` calls, or any
# .service file here, for the pattern).
#
# WHY: item A's postmortem (api/webull_stream.py's module docstring) -- an overnight
# Webull SDK reconnect storm filled __EDGELOG_HOME__/logs/qqq_exec.log to 203 MB /
# 1.1M lines in two days. Item A fixes that flood at its source (the SDK's own logger
# setup is disabled, and a dedup filter caps any repeat that still gets through); this
# is the belt-and-braces disk-usage backstop so ANY runaway logger -- this one or a
# future one, in any of these services -- can never fill the disk unbounded again.
#
# copytruncate is required, NOT the usual rename-then-signal rotation: every service
# here writes through systemd's own `StandardOutput=append:...` / `StandardError=
# append:...` (see e.g. edgelog-qqq-exec.service), which holds the log file open for
# the life of the unit -- there is no logger process to SIGHUP into reopening a
# renamed-away file, and restarting every service on every rotation just to reopen a
# handle would be worse than the problem it solves. copytruncate instead copies the
# current content out, then truncates the SAME inode in place, so the already-open
# handle keeps appending to it with no restart and no gap.
#
# su runs logrotate's own file access as the service user/group rather than root, to
# match the ownership these append-mode log files already have (created by each
# service's own `User=__EDGELOG_USER__`); this assumes that user's primary group
# shares its name, true for the default Ubuntu user this installer already relies on
# elsewhere (see install.sh's RUN_USER).
__EDGELOG_HOME__/logs/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 14
    maxsize 100M
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
    copytruncate
    su __EDGELOG_USER__ __EDGELOG_USER__
}
